@@ -35,11 +35,7 @@
 Pressure information for each resource is exported through the
 respective file in /proc/pressure/ -- cpu, memory, and io.
 
-The format for CPU is as such:
-
-some avg10=0.00 avg60=0.00 avg300=0.00 total=0
-
-and for memory and IO:
+The format for CPU, memory and IO is:
 
 some avg10=0.00 avg60=0.00 avg300=0.00 total=0
 full avg10=0.00 avg60=0.00 avg300=0.00 total=0
@@ -62,3 +58,48 @@
 tracked and exported as well, to allow detection of latency spikes
 which wouldn't necessarily make a dent in the time averages, or to
 average trends over custom time frames.
+
+Per-cgroup accounting and notifications
+======================================
+
+The cgroup v2 hierarchy exports cpu.pressure, memory.pressure and io.pressure
+for every group. Each group includes the pressure of its descendants; the root
+uses the system totals. CPU some/full also records a single runnable task waiting
+for its CPU quota or for a task in another cgroup. System-level CPU full has no
+useful definition and is exported as zero for ABI compatibility.
+
+To register a monitor, open a pressure file read/write and write a NUL-terminated
+string in the form "some <stall-us> <window-us>" or "full <stall-us> <window-us>".
+For example, "some 20000 500000" requests a notification after 20 milliseconds
+of stall in a 500 millisecond window. Windows range from 500,000 to 10,000,000
+microseconds, with a positive stall threshold no larger than the window.
+Keep this descriptor open and monitor POLLPRI or EPOLLPRI; closing it destroys
+the trigger. A second trigger write on the same descriptor returns EBUSY.
+POLLERR must not be treated as a pressure notification. The monitor limits
+notification frequency to one event per window.
+
+Writable /proc/pressure opens require CAP_SYS_RESOURCE. Writable delegated
+cgroup pressure files follow cgroup permissions; they do not apply that extra
+capability check in this backport. Administrators should account for per-group
+polling worker overhead when delegating writable pressure files.
+
+CONFIG_PSI=y enables accounting by default. Booting with psi=0 disables it.
+The optional CONFIG_PSI_DEFAULT_DISABLED reverses that default; psi=1 enables
+accounting explicitly. This backport does not add per-cgroup pressure toggles,
+IRQ pressure or the later unprivileged average-based monitoring API.
+
+BCM 4.19 backport scope
+======================
+
+The scheduler/group/trigger implementation is based on Linux v5.15.222 with
+additional upstream event, sampling, worker and lifetime corrections. Original
+task, cgroup, kernfs and worker layout is retained: a task bit uses existing
+space and kernel-private group/worker data is allocated after existing objects.
+
+Memory PSI retains the 4.19 reclaim and compaction annotations, including
+memory.high synchronous reclaim. It does not backport the complete workingset
+page-cache refault and swap-reload classification. Do not interpret zero memory
+pressure as evidence that no file/swap-related stall occurred. Merely marking
+all swap reads as memory pressure would introduce cold-swapin overcounting.
+Userspace policy, including systemd-oomd configuration, is separate from these
+kernel interfaces.
